From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM=1: while the pool is short or an area loads, an image takes no new 16 MiB block (a hole first, else memory of its own over 4 MiB or an 8 MiB block); EDEN_VMA_TRIM logs the slack by pool and the device memory freed; off by default

Not a port. PS5_Lichii keeps VMA's 16 MiB blocks and has no trim (its tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py,
read 10 Nov with gh api); its one other VMA change, the allocator's own budget instead of the
driver's double count, is our EDEN_PE_VMA_OWN_BUDGET (0312).

Why 684 MiB sat unused in the allocator's blocks (run739, t=89 s: 2931 MiB of blocks, 2247 in use):
- Not empty blocks. VMA 3.3 keeps at most one empty block per block vector (VmaBlockVector::Free:
  a block that empties is freed when another one is already empty, or at once when the heap's
  budget reads exceeded). The vectors with blocks here are VMA's own for memory types 0, 2, 3 and 5
  and the SparsePages and DeviceBuffers pools of type 0 (EDEN_REFUSED_VMA in earlier runs): at most
  6 x 16 MiB = 96 MiB, and none once the driver's double-counted budget reads exceeded. VMA has no
  call that frees that block sooner, so this patch does not try; the trim's own pool keeps at most
  one empty 8 MiB block.
- Not memory of its own. VMA counts a dedicated allocation as a block exactly as large as its use,
  so the 297 MiB image and everything else over half a block (8 MiB) adds no slack.
- Holes in the shared blocks. Images up to 8 MiB share 16 MiB blocks (VMA gives over half a block
  memory of its own), placed best fit (STRATEGY_MIN_MEMORY), and a block goes back only when every
  image in it is gone. At the door 619 images arrive in 23 frames, mixed into the same blocks as
  the new area's textures, and AREA_EVICT deletes about 2 GiB over the next ~180 frames (run739
  frames 3082-3266), many of them 2-8 MiB (frames 3247-3257: 11-13 images and 72-82 MiB a frame).
  Their places become holes that the neighbours which stay hold open. RADV_PS5_MEMORY at t=89: the
  allocator's 2931 MiB are 136 VkDeviceMemory objects, ~15 of them over 32 MiB (EDEN_POOL_LEDGER:
  1343 MiB in 15 larger), so the 684 MiB sit in at most ~120 shared blocks, a third or more of
  their space. Refusal reports of earlier runs show the same: type 0 with 400-1400 holes, the
  largest 8-15 MiB (a 16 MiB block held by one small image). Over 1872 refusal lines in 66 runs
  the slack was 216 MiB at the median (p75 293, max 547); only the earliest door refusal (hog
  1280, run761: 13 MiB) came before the evictions had made holes.
- Our budget/slack logic hides it rather than causes it: AllocatedUsage (vulkan_device.cpp) leaves
  the holes out of the caches' usage so the collector does not evict for them, and on the console
  the port measures usage by the pool. The collector deletes candidates in the emptiest blocks
  first (texture_cache.h), but only among images old enough; AREA_EVICT goes by age alone.

What it does (MemoryAllocator::CreateImage, before vmaCreateImage, while the trim is active):
1. The image is made (vkCreateImage) and its memory requirements read.
2. Space the blocks already hold: vmaAllocateMemoryForImage with NEVER_ALLOCATE, in the memory type
   the usual path takes first (no new memory, so outside the budget too). For images up to 8 MiB
   this is what vmaCreateImage does first anyway (VmaBlockVector::AllocatePage searches the blocks
   before it makes one); it is new only for larger ones, which VMA gives memory of their own first.
   The change is in steps 3 and 4: what finds no hole takes no new 16 MiB block.
3. None fits and it needs over half a small block (EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BLOCK_MIB, 2/4/8, unset 8, at
   most half of EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB): memory of its own (DEDICATED, within the budget, any type, as
   the usual path asks for it). It goes back to the pool when the texture cache deletes the image.
4. Otherwise a small block of a pool of its own (Device::SeparatePool::TrimmedImages, blocks of
   EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BLOCK_MIB), a new one only within the budget. A block one image holds open
   holds 8 MiB, not 16.
5. Anything refused: the memory and the image are let go and the usual path makes it with all its
   fallbacks (outside the budget, host memory, unbacked, on the console the port's
   ReleaseRefused). Huge images, those asked for `dedicated`, sparse, disjoint, lazily allocated,
   external and DRM-modifier ones never take this path.
The memory type: vmaAllocateMemoryForImage cannot use VMA_MEMORY_USAGE_AUTO*, so it asks with
UNKNOWN and the same preferred flag; VMA's cost (device-local preferred, uncached not preferred)
then picks the type vmaCreateImage picks for an image the host never touches (type 0, 0x1, here).

Active: the pool's largest free block under EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_MIB (unset 2048; off again over 1.5x),
read at most every 100 ms through the front's eden_free_memory_bytes (weak, ELF only; the mig020
front since 0503). Without it (the T88 front, other platforms) the trim goes by what the
allocator's budget leaves in the device-local heaps, which on the console is the driver's double
count: use the mig020 front. Also active for 10 s after EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BURST (unset 100; 0 off)
images were made within one second, an area load (AREA_EVICT's rule: 100 in 30 frames). Without
that, the door's images would be placed the usual way: run739's largest block fell from 5126 MiB
(t=81 s) to 612 (t=89 s), so most of the load came in above any mark.

Not thrashing at the door: nothing is moved and nothing is freed early; each image is allocated
once and freed when the texture cache deletes it, as before. More calls: one vkAllocateMemory /
vkFreeMemory pair per 4-8 MiB image that finds no hole (VMA's own path takes one per 16 MiB block),
and one per 8 MiB small block instead of per 16 MiB block. VMA keeps one empty block per pool, so
a small block is not made and freed as one image comes and goes, and the 1.5x way back keeps the
mark from flapping. Per image while active: vkGetImageMemoryRequirements and one to three VMA
calls (vmaCreateImage does one create and one allocation); every 100 ms the front's free memory
query (two kernel calls, as RefreshFreeMemory makes at 10 Hz). After the door the trim stays on
(run739's largest free block was 212-612 MiB from t=89 s on, under the mark), and memory of its own
has no empty-block hysteresis: a 4-8 MiB image the caches remake every few frames with no hole free
is one vkAllocateMemory / vkFreeMemory pair each time. The test reads that (Churn line, taken=).
Budget: run739's env reads the driver's budget (no EDEN_PE_VMA_OWN_BUDGET). VMA 3.3 checks
WITHIN_BUDGET only for memory of its own; a new block needs the budget's room whatever the flag.
Once the double count reads used up, the usual path already gives every image outside a hole
memory of its own (PS5_Lichii's note: 926 allocations), and the trim's steps 3-4 fall back
(fallback= rises): the trim acts only while the driver's budget still has room.

Trade-off: memory of its own of 2 MiB or more starts on a 2 MiB boundary of the pool
(radv_ps5_memory_alloc, the note in 0158), so a 5 MiB image leaves a 1 MiB tail only blocks under
2 MiB can use (EDEN_POOL_LEDGER's "under 2 MiB" may grow by that). That is why the default sends
only images over 4 MiB to memory of their own; EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BLOCK_MIB=4 sends all over 2 MiB.

Interplay: no guest memory, swap, JIT, heap or ahead-guard code is touched. The heap rescue and
EDEN_UP_HEAP_WAIT_MS are unchanged; a refused image reaches the port's ReleaseRefused through the
usual path as before. The census (0517) sees the trim's blocks in vma_blocks / vma_used
(vmaGetHeapBudgets counts pools). The collector's block order (texture_cache.h, MemoryBlock) sees
the small blocks at their own size and memory of its own in the middle, as before.
EDEN_PS5_TRANSIENT_POOLS images use their own pool first; only what falls back to CreateImage meets
the trim.

Lines (stderr.log; MiB, count/MiB):
  EDEN_VMA_TRIM ready: on under 2048 MiB of largest free block (off over 3072), small blocks 8 MiB,
      own memory over 4 MiB, burst 100 images/s, the figure from the pool|the allocator's budget
  EDEN_VMA_TRIM on|off largest= mark= burst=<bursts>[+ while one is on] blocks= used= slack=
      (+/-n since on) shared= sparse= buffers= trim= (blocks/slack each) holes= own= large= small=
      fallback= freed= taken=
when it turns on or off, and while on every 2 s with images placed (else every 20 s). slack: the
allocator's blocks less their use, all heaps; shared: VMA's own blocks, then the SparsePages,
DeviceBuffers and TrimmedImages pools (vmaGetPoolStatistics; the pools count alignment padding as
used, so shared is a few MiB high). freed / taken: all device memory the allocator gave back to /
took from the driver since the last line (its callbacks, for any reason): the freed MiB. The line
is written by the thread making the image (the GPU thread), which makes every other allocator call
there too; the allocator is externally synchronized and the census thread only reads budgets.

Expected (a guess, nothing measured): after the door (run739 t=89 s) slack ~250-350 MiB instead of
684, so ~300-400 MiB more of the pool free; at a refusal ~100-150 MiB more than the 216 MiB median
leaves, minus up to ~1 MiB of 2 MiB tail per "own" image. Whether the largest free block grows by
that much depends on where the freed blocks sit. A lower guess is as likely: run275 (T59, new game,
EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB=8, the same placement all the time) still showed 333-389 MiB slack in its heavy
scenes, and the own images' tails move part of the slack into the pool instead of freeing it; so
~150-300 MiB more largest free block. Not helped, and slightly worse: a refusal during the load
itself, before AREA_EVICT has made holes (run761, slack 13 MiB), where the usual path packs a new
16 MiB block tight and an own image leaves its tail. Not helped: slack in the DeviceBuffers or
SparsePages pools (the line's buffers= and sparse= say whether that is the next step).

Switches: EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"). EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_MIB,
EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BLOCK_MIB and EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BURST are read only with it on. Unset: CreateImage
reads one cached flag and calls vmaCreateImage as before; the device installs the same memory
callbacks as before (the churn ones with EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_CHURN, else none); GetSeparatePool's new
block size argument defaults to the old 16 MiB, the third pool row is never made, and
AddSeparatePoolUsage is only called by the trim's line.

Checks: not compiled, not run. git apply --check on T88 (ours-core-full); the same with 0493-0519
applied first, and 0493-0519 apply and skip the same with this applied first (none touches these
four files). The 24 literal prepare-vulkan-port.py anchors in vulkan_memory_allocator.cpp/.h and
vulkan_device.cpp match as before (mig020-front and fable-tricks-16 copies); the port does not call
GetSeparatePool. VMA 3.3.0 calls checked against its header: vmaAllocateMemoryForImage,
vmaBindImageMemory, vmaFindMemoryTypeIndex, vmaGetMemoryProperties, vmaGetHeapBudgets,
vmaGetPoolStatistics; NEVER_ALLOCATE never with DEDICATED, DEDICATED never with a pool (VMA refuses
both pairs). The fable-swap core (older, no churn callbacks) does not take it.

PS5 test (the a1-walk-fast door run on run739's env, mig020 front, RADV_PS5_MEMORY_REPORT=1, and
EDEN_UP_MEM_CENSUS=1 with 0517 in the build, EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_CHURN=1 in every arm (its Churn line
counts the allocator's vkAllocateMemory / vkFreeMemory with the trim on or off); EDEN_UP_HEAP_RESCUE
off, since with it hog 1024-1280 ended in SIGSEGV on CPUCore_4; same-batch control, about 3 valid
runs per arm, two batches of 5):
  A  EDEN_TEST_POOL_HOG_MIB=1152                     control (1024 lived in run759, 1280 died at 91 s)
  B  EDEN_TEST_POOL_HOG_MIB=1152 EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM=1
  D  EDEN_TEST_POOL_HOG_MIB=1152 EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB=8  the existing switch (0158; one run so far,
     run275, never on the door): the trim's placement all the time, no new code. If D matches B,
     ship the env line instead of this patch.
  later: EDEN_UP_VMA_TRIM_BLOCK_MIB=4 in B; hog 1024 with the trim against its own same-batch
  control; hog 1280 dies during the load itself (slack 13 MiB), which this does not address.
Read: slack at the first EDEN_VMA_USAGE after the door (B and D under 350 against A's ~600),
EDEN_MEMORY's largest free block at t~90 and t~110, crash or not and when, EDEN_VMA_TRIM fallback=
(near 0; high means the driver's budget read used up) and taken= / freed= counts (tens per 2 s
line at the door, not hundreds; freed MiB is large at the door, that is AREA_EVICT), the Churn
line's device allocations after the door against A, shared= against buffers= slack, RADV app
objects (136 in run739; a few hundred is fine, thousands is thrash), EDEN_POOL_LEDGER "under
2 MiB", and the door's frame times and stutter in play-report.py against A.

